post.description:
**Start here to send mail.** Of the routes that put a message somewhere, this is the one an application wants: JSON in, the server builds the RFC 5322 message, and you get one result per recipient.

Reach for a different route when:
- you are answering an existing conversation — `POST /mailboxes/{mailboxId}/threads/{threadId}/reply` derives the recipient and the threading headers for you;
- you want to store a message without sending it (a draft, a seeded thread) — `POST /mailboxes/{mailboxId}/messages/compose`;
- you already hold raw RFC 5322 bytes to file into the mailbox (a migration, an IMAP APPEND) — `POST /mailboxes/{mailboxId}/messages`;
- you are a submission agent wanting one verdict per RCPT — `POST /deliver/outbound`.

Answers `200` when every recipient was accepted and `207` when the outcome is mixed: a status line cannot express three verdicts, so the `recipients` array is the contract. Retries are cheap and correct — per-recipient idempotency keys are derived from your `X-Delivery-Id`, so resubmitting the same request re-converges only what failed.
